# THE SMALLEST THING THAT PROVES THE VOCABULARY RUNS A GUEST.
#
# NOT A DISTRIBUTION INSTALL. Most of what this repository's walkthroughs do needs a full machine -
# UEFI firmware, a virtio disk, a VGA adapter, a USB tablet - because they drive an INSTALLER through
# its GUI. This drives a guest that boots in under a minute on 512 MiB, so it is the file that should
# run on every push: a vocabulary change that breaks `click`, `see`, `want` or the dispatcher fails
# here in ninety seconds rather than forty minutes into a kubuntu install.
#
# A MICROVM HAS NO FIRMWARE AND NO DISPLAY, so the two things a normal boot relies on are both gone:
# qemu is handed the kernel directly, and the guest's only output is the serial line. That is why
# every assertion below reads the CONSOLE rather than the screen.

walkthrough "alpine microvm - the vocabulary against the smallest guest"
  resolution 1024x768
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  needs alpine.iso alpine-vmlinuz alpine-initramfs

  # THE MEDIUM IS ATTACHED AS A SECOND DISK, not booted from: a microvm is handed its kernel, and
  # Alpine's `modloop=` points at the squashfs inside this ISO. MEASURED without it: the initramfs
  # mounts nothing and drops to `initramfs emergency recovery shell launched`.
  make a disk "alpine.qcow2" of 1 GiB
  boot as a microvm from kernel "alpine-vmlinuz" and initramfs "alpine-initramfs", waiting up to 300s

  # THE GUEST SAYS THIS ITSELF, on the serial line, before anything else exists. It is the machine
  # saying "a kernel ran", which is the first fact this walkthrough establishes.
  wait for the console to say "Linux version", waiting up to 180s

  # AND THEN IT SAYS THIS, which is Alpine's own way of saying "init finished and I want a login".
  # This is the fact the whole file exists to establish: the vocabulary started a guest and the
  # guest came all the way up.
  the console will say "login"

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
